 With large speech corpora and the development of sequence-to-sequence models, recent text-to-speech(TTS) models have achieved human-like speech synthesis at a level comparable to human speech utterances #ref1# #ref2# #ref3#. Multi-speaker TTS can synthesize speech with the desired speaker characteristics by inputting speaker information in addition to text #ref4# #ref5# #ref6#. TTS training typically requires pairs  of text and speech, which are called text-speech pairs. However, the cost of collecting a large amount of high-quality speech data is very high. As a result, the number of speakers and the amount of speech data are limited, making it difficult to increase the variation of speakers in the data. In contrast, there is a large amount of data whose availability is unknown, such as YouTube videos, and we call such data ``dark data.'' In this study, we propose a method to select dark data for TTS training.

The main problem of TTS training from dark data is the noise in the speech. The speech in dark data often contains background noise and errors in phoneme recognition. If the noise is too high, the synthetic speech quality will be degraded. Therefore, previous studies have selected dark data on the basis of acoustic quality. In #ref7#, the authors proposed an acoustic-quality-based data selection method for TTS training. The method estimates the acoustic quality of each utterance from dark data using a pre-trained acoustic quality estimator. The utterances are then ranked and selected according to their acoustic quality. In #ref8#, the authors proposed a method to select dark data by combining the acoustic quality and the confidence of the phoneme recognition result. In #ref9#, the authors proposed a method to select dark data on the basis of the distance between the acoustic features of the utterances and the feature distribution of the TTS training data. However, the TTS model can generate high-quality synthetic speech even if the noise in the speech is high. Therefore, the quality of the speech itself is not the only factor that affects the quality of the synthetic speech. It is important to select dark data on the basis of the importance of the data to the given TTS model.

In this paper, we propose a method to select dark data for TTS training on the basis of the importance of the data to the given TTS model. Our method uses a loop of training and evaluation. In the loop, we train the TTS model with the dark data selected by the data selection method and evaluate the quality of the synthetic speech generated from the trained model. The method then updates the importance of the data in the dark data on the basis of the quality of the synthetic speech. The training and evaluation are repeated until the data selection converges.

The contributions of this paper are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We propose a method to select training data for TTS from dark data. The method selects data on the basis of the importance of the data to the given TTS model, not the quality of the speech itself.
\item We propose an evaluation-in-the-loop data selection method. The method updates the importance of the data in the dark data on the basis of the quality of the synthetic speech.
\end{itemize}

